WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.500
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:09.420
I get a lot of messages from you Fellow Scholars
that you would like to get started in machine

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.920
learning and are looking for materials. Words
fail to describe how great the feeling is

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:19.360
that the series inspires many of you to start
your career in research.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.460
At this point, we're not only explaining the
work of research scientists but creating new

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.330
research scientists.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:31.180
Machine learning is an amazing field of research
that provides us with incredible tools that

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:35.500
help us solve problems that were previously
impossible to solve.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.410
Neural networks can paint in the style of
famous artists or recognize images and are

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:44.969
capable of so many other things it simply
blows my mind.

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:50.380
However, bear in mind that machine learning
is not an easy field. This field fuses together

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:55.860
the beauty, rigor, and preciseness of mathematics
with the useful applications of engineering.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:01.930
It is also a fast moving field, on almost
any given day, 10 new scientific papers pop

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.739
up in the repositories.

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:07.630
For everything that I mention in this video
there is a link in the description box and

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:11.500
more, so make sure to dive in and check
them out. If you have other materials that

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:16.060
helped you understand some of the more difficult
concepts, please let me know in the comments

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.729
section and I'll include them in the text
below.

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:24.229
First, some non-scientific texts to get you
in the mood, I recommend reading "The Road

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:30.259
to Superintelligence" on a fantastic blog
by the name Wait But Why. This is a frighteningly

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:35.409
long article for many, but I guarantee that you won't be able to stop reading it.

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:35.979
Beware.

00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:41.320
Nick Bostrom's Superintelligence is also a
fantastic read, after which you'll probably

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.970
be convinced that it doesn't make sense to
work on anything else but machine learning.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:50.759
There is a previous Two Minute Papers episode
on artificial superintelligence if you're

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:52.799
looking for a teaser for this book.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.460
Now let's get a bit more technical with some of the better video series and courses out there!

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:04.140
Welch labs is an amazing YouTube channel
with a very intuitive introduction to the

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.579
concept of neural networks.
Andrew Ng is a Chief Scientist at Baidu

00:02:09.579 --> 00:02:14.489
Research in deep learning. His wonderful course
is widely regarded as the pinnacle of all

00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:17.939
machine learning courses and is therefore
highly recommended.

00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:23.879
Nando de Freitas is a professor at the university
of Oxford, and has also worked with DeepMind.

00:02:23.879 --> 00:02:28.099
His course that he held at the University
of British Columbia covers many of the more

00:02:28.099 --> 00:02:30.819
advanced concepts in machine learning.

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:36.340
Regarding books, I recommend reading my favorite
Holy Tome of machine learning, that goes by

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:41.529
the name of "Pattern Recognition and Machine
Learning" by Christoper Bishop. A sample chapter

00:02:41.529 --> 00:02:46.430
is available from the book if you wish to
take a look. It has beautiful typesetting,

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:51.999
lots of intuition and crystal clear presentation.
Definitely worth every penny of the price.

00:02:51.999 --> 00:02:56.260
I'd like to note that I am not paid for any
of the book endorsements in the series. When

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:01.969
I recommend a book, I genuinely think that it provides
great value to you Fellow Scholars.

00:03:01.969 --> 00:03:06.699
About software libraries. Usually, in most
fields, the main problem is that the

00:03:06.699 --> 00:03:12.549
implementation of many state of the art techniques
are severely lacking. Well luckily, in the

00:03:12.549 --> 00:03:16.689
machine learning community, we have them in
abundance. I've linked a great talk on what

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:21.279
libraries are available and the strengths
and weaknesses for each of them.

00:03:21.279 --> 00:03:26.279
At this point, you'll probably have an idea
of which direction you're most excited about.

00:03:26.279 --> 00:03:31.120
Start searching for keywords, make sure to
read the living hell out of the machine learning

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:37.840
reddit to stay up to date, and, the best part is yet to come: starting to explore on your own.

00:03:37.919 --> 00:03:41.230
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

